WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.676
Hi, I'm Pintle!

00:00:02.676 --> 00:00:06.216
Earshot fit takes a recording and writes a Python recipe that

00:00:06.216 --> 00:00:07.504
makes the sound again.

00:00:07.504 --> 00:00:10.722
Here is how it works, with no neural network inside.

00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:15.823
Fit small synthesis models with parameters a sound designer would name.

00:00:15.823 --> 00:00:16.884
Render every one.

00:00:16.884 --> 00:00:19.005
Keep the one that sounds closest.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.364
First it measures the sound.

00:00:22.364 --> 00:00:25.491
Attack, decay, how tonal it is, whether the pitch glides.

00:00:25.491 --> 00:00:27.992
That names a class and orders the candidates.

00:00:28.412 --> 00:00:30.248
Then three classic fitters run.

00:00:30.248 --> 00:00:34.655
Damped modes for struck things, tracked partials for tones, and a banded

00:00:34.655 --> 00:00:36.124
noise gesture for whooshes.

00:00:36.124 --> 00:00:37.226
Hybrids stack them.

00:00:37.646 --> 00:00:40.672
Each candidate is rendered and scored against the recording.

00:00:41.092 --> 00:00:43.685
The lowest score is written out as a recipe module.

00:00:45.442 --> 00:00:46.566
Here is the formula.

00:00:47.442 --> 00:00:50.533
The mel number is the spectrogram distance in decibels.

00:00:50.953 --> 00:00:54.271
Tone is the balance across third-octave bands, weighted lightly.

00:00:54.691 --> 00:00:57.969
And r is how well the loudness contours line up.

00:00:57.969 --> 00:00:59.935
Onset and leftover energy terms follow.

00:01:01.608 --> 00:01:03.411
The file it writes is mostly data.

00:01:03.919 --> 00:01:07.798
A model literal, with each partial as a few frequency and level

00:01:07.798 --> 00:01:09.091
breakpoints, plus noise bands.

00:01:09.511 --> 00:01:11.527
Defaults that re-render the fit exactly.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:15.635
And knobs, like transpose, decay and brightness, to make new takes.

00:01:16.055 --> 00:01:19.831
Render hands it all to the earshot engine, so it needs earshot installed.

00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:25.010
On a shared CPU, a synthetic struck bar came back almost exactly,

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:26.949
and its three modes were found.

00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:29.210
The pitch glide was the weakest fit.

00:01:30.392 --> 00:01:32.993
The code is a readable parameter set with knobs.

00:01:32.993 --> 00:01:35.306
The knobs, not the bytes, are the point.

00:01:37.942 --> 00:01:39.004
Measure, fit, render, score.

00:01:39.004 --> 00:01:41.660
What you get back is a sound you can turn.

00:01:42.542 --> 00:01:44.670
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.278
I'm Pintle.

00:01:45.278 --> 00:01:45.582
Bye!
